#heading(numbering: none)[Abstract]
Robot brains today are either hand-built pipelines or single opaque networks.
Biology suggests a third shape: separate organs on typed pathways, innate drives,
learning from surprise, and sleep. We present IPSE, a humanoid brain built that way
on a Booster K1, and add what biology cannot do: learned networks are inherited
directly by the next robot. Every organ is a pure step function on a typed bus, so
the same organ code runs live, from tape, in simulation and in overnight dreams, and
every organ's inputs and outputs are recorded as training data. A surprise stream —
one-step physics prediction from the motor efference copy, minus what was sensed —
drives attention, global neuromodulators, and prioritized sleep replay. We
pre-register three claims: surprise detects falls, actuator faults and drift at
least as well as dedicated detectors (E1); surprise-prioritized sleep improves the
next day (E2); and a robot booted with an inherited epigenome reaches competence
faster than one learning from scratch (E3). _Results: TBD._
